 ## Queue and Tickets
 
+### Queue
+
+Each queue belongs to **one** entity and has:
+
+- A `name` and a `slug` that is unique within the entity.
+- An `is_active` flag (default `true`).
+- Optional `labels_i18n` (JSON) for localized queue labels.
+
+Issuance into an inactive queue is rejected (`App\Exceptions\InactiveQueueException`).
+
 ### Ticket
 
-Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call** — all data is immutable after issuance, except `called_at` and `called_by` (filled when called). Attributes:
+Unit of service in the queue. It is **single-use** and receives **one single call** — all data is immutable after issuance, except `status`, `called_at` and `called_by_user_id` (filled when called). Attributes:
 
 - Source queue — the ticket belongs to **one** queue (within the entity); does not record which totem it came from.
+- Display `code` — per-queue sequence `A001`, `A002`, … (prefix `A`, at least 3 digits, zero-padded; grows past 999 as `A1000`, `A1001`, …). Unique per `(queue_id, code)`.
 - Priority (numeric, see below).
 - Issuance timestamp (ordering "oldest first" within same priority).
-- Issuance channel: totem, virtual, printed copy, etc.
+- Issuance channel: `totem`, `virtual` (default), `printed`.
+- Status: `waiting` (new tickets) → `called` (no cancelled status).
+- Call fields `called_at` and `called_by_user_id` (nullable FK to users).
+
+### Issuance and calling (implemented)
+
+- `App\Actions\IssueTicket` issues a ticket into a queue with a priority (`>= 0`, default `0`) and a channel (default `virtual`). A negative priority is rejected with `InvalidArgumentException`.
+- The queue state is re-read from the database inside the transaction (row lock), so a queue deactivated after the caller loaded it is still rejected. Nothing is persisted on rejection. Unique `(queue_id, code)` collisions (concurrent issuance) are retried a few times.
+- The next display code is "last valid code + 1" by insertion order (not string order, so `A999` then `A1000` yields `A1001`); deleting a ticket does not reuse its number (after `A001`..`A003` with `A002` deleted, the next is `A004`). Malformed legacy codes (anything not matching `A<digits>`) are skipped: with only `A-X` present the next is `A001`; with `A001` then `A-X` the next is `A002`.
+- `Ticket::nextCode(int $queueId)` computes the next code; `Ticket::call(User $user)` marks a waiting ticket as called exactly once, recording `called_at` and the calling user. Only members of the ticket's entity may call (others: `Illuminate\Auth\Access\AuthorizationException`, ticket unchanged); calling a non-waiting ticket throws `LogicException`. Selection of *which* ticket to call next (priority/fairness) is a separate concern.
 
